> I'm now working on that (after a while without working on lojban).
> However, is the =93o=94 sound correct here? It points to a close-mid back
> unrounded vowel (no english example on the Wikipedia page), but I'm almos=
t
> certain that it should be a rounded one (like in =93y_aw_n=94, if I belie=
ve
> Wikipedia). I've used that latter sound in the Wave lesson, but it's a tw=
o
> letters change if I'm wrong ;-)
>

I changed it to Mid back rounded vowel in the article, which I'm pretty
sure is what I had originally, someone must have changed it to Close-mid
back unrounded. I also changed "e" to Mid front unrounded. Both rounded and
unrounded are allowed, as there is no specification of roundedness for
vowels, but if you must go one way I'd go with rounded o and unrounded e.
It also doesn't need to be Close-mid. The specification for "o" is "a back
mid vowel" and for "e" it's "a front mid vowel".


Also, about the =93r=94 (I have a long story with the =93r=94 ;-)), it poin=
ts to an
> alveolar tap. I thought it was an alveolar trill! My mistake (if it is on=
e)
> comes from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lojban#Phonology_and_orthography=
],
> I guess, where an alveolar trill is given for the =93r=94. And it had som=
ething
> to do with the spanish =93r=94, too, but I don't remember where I did rea=
d
> that, and anyway, on [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R], we can see that
> both sound are used in spanish (so, it was not precise, but not false to =
do
> that kind of comparison). Well, I would like to know what is the =93best=
=94
> sound (as the two are correct, thank to lojban laxism about sounds
> differentiation). First, to point it as an example, second, to correct an=
y
> =93prefered way=94 that I could have put in the french translation (as th=
e
> french =93r=94 is really different from both sounds, I spent some time on=
 it).
>

Many are allowed, but the alveolar tap is probably the most common across
languages, and so I think it's the one that should be preferred.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

